---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import RecapChart from "../components/RecapChart.astro";

const title = "Work | Financing, Finance, Structure | MENADD";
const description =
  "What I work on with founders between pre-seed and Series A: structure and ownership, finance, a financing decision, a move across borders.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description} canonical="/work/" ogImage="/og/shared.jpg" ogImageAlt="Gennady Shafir, CFO and board advisor" wide>
  <div class="work-page twelve-column-grid">
    <section class="work-intro">
      <h1>What I work on</h1>
      <p class="lede">Founders usually come to me in one of four situations between pre-seed and Series A. The work starts with the documents and the numbers. It ends with a decision you can defend to an investor.</p>
      <div class="work-stage-map">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Stage</th><th>What usually needs fixing</th><th>Where</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Pre-seed</td><td>Founder split and vesting. First SAFEs. Which entity to raise into.</td><td><a href="#structure">Structure</a></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Seed</td><td>ESOP. IP ownership. A cap table that matches the documents. Cash reporting.</td><td><a href="#structure">Structure</a>, <a href="#finance">Finance</a></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Towards Series A</td><td>The model. The data room. The term sheet and board terms.</td><td><a href="#finance">Finance</a>, <a href="#financing">Financing</a></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Any stage</td><td>A holding or a headquarters that has to move.</td><td><a href="#border">Borders</a></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="work-sections">
      <section id="structure">
        <h2>Structure and ownership</h2>
        <p><strong>When:</strong> Before the first priced round, or when diligence is close.</p>
        <p>Operational is not the same as investable. A structure built for licensing, banking and invoicing rarely answers an investor's questions.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Founder equity: the split, vesting, and what happens when a founder leaves.</li>
          <li>Employee equity: an ESOP investors accept, options promised but never granted, phantom equity where a plan was expected.</li>
          <li>SAFEs and convertibles: what they convert into, together, at the next round.</li>
          <li>The entity and the IP: which company the round closes into, and whether it owns what it sells.</li>
          <li>The cap table record: reconciled to signed documents and, where it helps, moved to Carta.</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>You get:</strong> A short memo with what has to change and in what order, and the change run with your counsel until it is done.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="finance">
        <h2>Finance the company has outgrown</h2>
        <p><strong>When:</strong> Revenue is real and the founder still runs cash in a spreadsheet.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Reporting that runs without a finance team: bank and accounting data, with simple AI tools on top that produce weekly cash and runway.</li>
          <li>The metrics an investor will recalculate anyway.</li>
          <li>The financial model, the part of this work I have done most: assumptions an investor can test, scenarios the board can run the company on.</li>
          <li>Board and investor reporting on a fixed rhythm.</li>
          <li>The data room and the diligence answers.</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>You get:</strong> Numbers produced every week, not rebuilt for every investor meeting. This is ongoing work inside the team, not a report.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="financing">
        <h2>A financing decision</h2>
        <p><strong>When:</strong> A term sheet, a new convertible, a bridge or a down round is on the table.</p>
        <p>The terms look reasonable one by one. Their combined effect shows up two rounds later.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Ownership round by round, issued and fully diluted.</li>
          <li>Control: board seats, veto rights, voting thresholds, drag-along.</li>
          <li>Down-round protection, and who it lands on.</li>
          <li>Exit distributions at several sale values, under the preference terms in the documents.</li>
          <li>Competing proposals, side by side.</li>
        </ul>
        <RecapChart />
        <p><strong>You get:</strong> An editable model, a short memo with the points to negotiate, and a walkthrough.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="border">
        <h2>A move across borders</h2>
        <p><strong>When:</strong> The holding, the headquarters or the investors sit in a different country from the business.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>The target structure, designed from the capital stack backwards: ADGM, DIFC, Luxembourg or the BVI.</li>
          <li>What moves and in what order: IP, contracts, people, cash.</li>
          <li>Banking and tax, checked with advisers in each jurisdiction.</li>
          <li>The advisers themselves, coordinated until the structure is in place.</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>You get:</strong> A structure that works for banking, for tax and for the next investor at the same time.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>How it starts</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>A short form. You tell me the company, the decision and the deadline.</li>
          <li>A call. Thirty minutes. I ask most of the questions.</li>
          <li>A written scope. What I will do, by when, and the fee. Work starts when the scope is agreed.</li>
          <li>A first piece of work with a defined end. What follows depends on what we both see.</li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Ways to work together</h2>
        <p><strong>Inside the team.</strong> A senior finance role alongside the founder, for as long as the company needs it.</p>
        <p><strong>On the board.</strong> A board or advisory seat, with ownership and control kept in the room at every decision.</p>
        <p><strong>One defined piece of work.</strong> A structure audit, a finance setup or a term sheet analysis. Scope and fee agreed before you commit.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="work-limits">
        <p>I provide financial and commercial analysis and run the process. Legal drafting, opinions and filings stay with counsel. Nothing here promises a financing, a valuation or an investor introduction.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="work-cta">
        <h2>Tell me what is in front of you.</h2>
        <a class="button-link" href="/start/">Start a conversation</a>
      </section>

      <section class="work-reading">
        <h2>Further reading</h2>
        <p><a href="/library/ownership/how-founders-lose-a-company-in-three-years/">How Founders Lose a Company in Three Years</a></p>
        <p><a href="/library/control/control-shifts-quietly/">Valuation Makes Headlines. Control Shifts Quietly.</a></p>
        <p><a href="/library/structure/operational-is-not-investable/">Which Entity a Round Actually Closes Into</a></p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
